Explain Your Project
Interview Answer-

Jan 2025 till present -
Recently I worked on an AML (Anti Money Laundering) platform for one of my banking clients, supporting multiple regions including NAM, APAC, EMEA, LATAM, Mexico, and SK.
The platform used to monitor suspicious activities across capital market business lines including Foreign exchange, prime brokerage, and External entities.
The system used to process large-scale trade, transactional data for suspicious activity monitoring and compliance reporting.
we used Batch pipelines mainly for historical analysis, customer risk profiling, reconciliation, and regulatory reporting.
and Incrmental pipelines for suspicious activity monitoring and alert generation.
Data was ingested from enterprise source systems using Azure Data Factory and Autoloader.
Spark and Databricks were used for distributed processing, ETL pipelines, enrichment, AML rule execution, and Delta Lake processing.
The platform used Medallion Architecture with Bronze, Silver, and Gold layers for scalable and governed data processing.
My core responsibility was to design end-to-end data solutions for AML use cases, define architecture standards and best practices, and guide teams in building scalable, secure, and reliable data platforms.

**** source -> ADF (trigger pipeline time based or event based)-> Bronze layer(raw folder) -> SCD2 & other transformation & enrichments and load in silver layer -> risk scoring basis on typologies & defined thresholds, we generate alerts for customers and transactions -> create semantic views for further used by downstream users by securing region level access.*****

- Batch Ingestion (ADF → Bronze)
 ADF Copy Activity moves bulk data (e.g., daily extracts from SQL, CSV files) into the Bronze layer of your Data Lake.
 Triggered on a schedule (nightly, hourly, or event‑based).
 Ensures historical completeness and large‑volume ingestion.

- INcremental Ingestion 
•	Parameterised ADF Pipelines call REST APIs → dump JSON/XML payloads into ADLS Gen2.
•	Databricks Autoloader ingests raw API responses into Delta tables in the Bronze layer.
•	Data remains unaltered (raw, semi-structured) for traceability and compliance. → builds Silver (latest snapshot) -> SCD2 logic → builds Gold (historical lineage).

Source -
•	Core Banking Systems APIs
o	Customer account details, balances, transactions

In practice, you’d design parameterized ADF pipelines or Databricks notebooks that hit these REST endpoints, capture incremental loads (transactional data) and batch loads (reference data), and store them in the Bronze layer for downstream SCD2 and enrichment.

Components -
•	Linked Service (BankingAPI_DS) Defines the REST API endpoint (e.g., core banking, payments, CRM). 
•	Dataset (Bronze_ADLS_DS) Points to Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 in the Bronze layer. 
•	Copy Activity Moves raw JSON/XML payloads from the API → ADLS Bronze.
o	Source: REST API
o	Sink: JSON files in Bronze

Flow -
1.	ADF pipeline calls the REST API.
2.	Raw response (JSON/XML) is stored in ADLS Bronze.
3.	Databricks Autoloader picks up new files and writes them into Delta tables for downstream Silver/Gold processing
