3. Explain Project Use Cases
Important Understanding Before Use Cases
In AML systems, both historical analysis and streaming monitoring are used together.
Historical analysis focuses on analyzing past customer behavior and transaction history to identify unusual patterns over time.
Streaming monitoring focuses on detecting suspicious activities quickly as events arrive in near real-time.
The engineering team mainly builds scalable ingestion, ETL, enrichment, and AML processing pipelines while compliance/business teams define AML rules and thresholds.
________________________________________
Difference Between Transaction Events and Trade Events
Transaction Events:
•	money movement
•	payments
•	journal transfers
•	wire transfers
•	asset movement
Example: Customer transfers 500,000 USD between accounts.
Trade Events:
•	buy/sell financial instruments
•	FX trades
•	equity trades
•	bond trades
Example: Customer performs a large FX currency trade.
Both transaction events and trade events are monitored by AML systems because suspicious activity can happen in both areas.
________________________________________
Understanding Enterprise Source Systems
Data was ingested from multiple enterprise systems.
These are generic business terms because every bank may use different applications internally.
Examples:
Transaction Systems: Systems generating banking transaction data such as payments, wire transfers, journals, and account movements.
Trade Platforms: Systems generating FX trades, equity trades, asset transfers, and capital market activity.
Customer Master Systems: Systems storing customer profile, account, and KYC information.
Reference Data Systems: Systems storing country risk information, currency mappings, risk categories, and sanctions/watchlist data.
External Compliance Sources: Third-party compliance and sanctions data providers.
________________________________________
Understanding Data Ingestion
Real enterprise data exists across multiple systems.
Examples:
•	SQL Server/Oracle databases
•	APIs
•	flat files like CSV/Parquet
•	streaming event systems
•	enterprise applications
ADF pipelines were mainly used for batch ingestion from databases, APIs, and files.
Azure Event Hubs was used for streaming ingestion of trade and transaction events.
The ingested data was stored in Bronze layer Delta tables before further processing.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Explain Project Use Cases
Use Case 1 — Historical Analysis
Interview Answer
One major use case was historical transaction analysis for AML compliance monitoring.
The platform analyzed large volumes of historical trade and transaction data to identify unusual customer behavior, suspicious transaction patterns, and high-risk activities.
For example, if a customer suddenly performed unusually large FX trades or rapid asset transfers compared to historical behavior, the system generated a higher risk score.
Batch pipelines processed historical data across multiple regions and business lines to support compliance reporting, customer risk profiling, and regulatory investigations.
________________________________________
Use Case 2 — Streaming Alert System
Interview Answer
Another important use case was near real-time suspicious activity monitoring.
Trade and transaction events were streamed through Azure Event Hubs and processed using Spark Structured Streaming in Databricks.
The streaming pipeline applied AML validation rules, risk scoring logic, and suspicious activity detection.
For example, if unusually high-value FX trades or rapid journal movements were detected from high-risk entities, the system generated compliance alerts within near real-time.
The alerts were consumed by downstream compliance systems and monitoring dashboards.
________________________________________
Follow-Up Questions
Q1. Why streaming was needed?
Answer:
Some suspicious activities required faster response for compliance and fraud monitoring, so near real-time processing helped generate alerts quickly.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Conflict within the team?
Yes, I faced a situation where two senior engineers disagreed on onboarding a new source.
One wanted a quick custom pipeline to meet the deadline, while the other wanted a reusable framework for future scalability.
I brought them together and reviewed the trade-offs. Since our existing pipelines were stable, we decided not to touch them. Instead, we built a lightweight reusable framework only for new sources.
This helped us meet the timeline, avoid production risk, and make future onboarding easier.
It was a good example of balancing short-term delivery with long-term architecture goals.
